    "lessons": [
      {
        "id": "m1-theory",
        "title": "Short Vowels — How They Work",
        "type": "theory",
        "order": 1,
        "theoryHtml": "<h2>Short Vowels</h2><p>English has five core short vowels. Each lives in a different part of your mouth.</p><ul><li><strong>/æ/</strong> — <em>cat</em>. Jaw drops, tongue flat and forward.</li><li><strong>/ɛ/</strong> — <em>bed</em>. Mid-front, relaxed.</li><li><strong>/ɪ/</strong> — <em>sit</em>. High-front, lax.</li><li><strong>/ɒ/</strong> — <em>hot</em>. Low-back, rounded lips.</li><li><strong>/ʌ/</strong> — <em>cup</em>. Central, neutral lips.</li></ul><p>The key is <strong>not</strong> to extend these sounds into diphthongs — keep them short and crisp.</p>",
        "words": []
      },
      {
