      {
        "id": "m7-theory",
        "title": "Voiced & Voiceless Pairs",
        "type": "theory",
        "order": 1,
        "theoryHtml": "<h2>Consonant Pairs</h2><p>English has three major stop consonant pairs where the only difference is voicing.</p><ul><li><strong>P/B</strong> — Both are bilabial stops. /p/ is voiceless (air only), /b/ adds vocal vibration.</li><li><strong>T/D</strong> — Both are alveolar stops. /t/ is voiceless, /d/ is voiced.</li><li><strong>K/G</strong> — Both are velar stops. /k/ is voiceless, /g/ is voiced.</li></ul><p><strong>Aspiration:</strong> In English, voiceless stops at the start of a stressed syllable get a puff of air — <em>pat</em> = /pʰæt/. Put your hand in front of your mouth to feel it.</p>",
        "words": []
      },
