        "id": "m8-theory",
        "title": "Consonant Clusters — No Shortcuts",
        "type": "theory",
        "order": 1,
        "theoryHtml": "<h2>Consonant Clusters</h2><p>English allows up to three consonants before the vowel in a syllable. Every consonant must be audible.</p><ul><li><strong>str-</strong> — <em>street, strong, stream</em>. Three distinct sounds: /s/ + /t/ + /ɹ/.</li><li><strong>spl-</strong> — <em>splash, split</em>. /s/ + /p/ + /l/.</li><li><strong>scr-</strong> — <em>screen, scroll</em>. /s/ + /k/ + /ɹ/.</li><li><strong>spr-</strong> — <em>spring, spread</em>. /s/ + /p/ + /ɹ/.</li></ul><p><strong>Common error:</strong> inserting a vowel between consonants (<em>e-street</em> → /ɪstriːt/). Practise slowly, then speed up.</p>",
