      {
        "id": "m9-theory",
        "title": "Flap T — The Heart of American English",
        "type": "theory",
        "order": 1,
        "theoryHtml": "<h2>Flap T &amp; Linking</h2><p>In American English, the /t/ between two vowels (especially unstressed ones) becomes a rapid tap — almost like a /d/. This is called the <strong>flap-T</strong> (/ɾ/).</p><ul><li><em>butter</em> → /ˈbʌɾər/ (sounds like \"budder\")</li><li><em>water</em> → /ˈwɔːɾər/</li><li><em>better</em> → /ˈbɛɾər/</li></ul><p>The flap is produced with a single very quick tongue tap against the alveolar ridge. This is what makes American English sound fluid rather than clipped.</p><p>In words ending in unstressed <strong>-ty</strong>, the same flap applies: <em>city</em> → /ˈsɪɾi/, <em>pretty</em> → /ˈpɹɪɾi/.</p>",
